# Find by name within an industry, sorted by the share of gross profit that costs take
async function testMain_indexFind():
    data = testData(['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'])
    systemGlobalSet('vType', 'all')
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', 'costs')
    systemGlobalSet('vSic', 7372)
    systemGlobalSet('vFind', 'CO')
    unittestMockAll(data)
    tiMain()
    log = unittestMockEnd()
    systemGlobalSet('vType', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vSort', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vSic', null)
    systemGlobalSet('vFind', null)
    unittestDeepEqual(log, [ \
        [ \
            'systemFetch', \
            [ \
                ['data/meta.json', 'data/index.json'] \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                'Index | [Group Totals](#var.vPage=\'totals\'&_top) | [Methodology](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top) | [Data and Limits](#var.vPage=\'data\'&_top)' \
            ] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'documentSetTitle', \
            ['The Treadmill Index'] \
        ], \
        [ \
            'markdownPrint', \
            [ \
                '', \
                '# The Treadmill Index', \
                '', \
                'Large, growing companies whose revenue growth has not become meaningful cash for outside owners, because the cash', \
                'goes out as stock pay. Owner cash is free cash flow less stock-based compensation; "meaningful" is 3.0% of revenue.', \
                'Of 11 companies in the SEC data, 5 pass the screen and 4 are ranked. [How it is computed](#var.vPage=\'method\'&_top).', \
                '', \
                '## What a High Rank Can Mean for a Shareholder', \
                '', \
                '**The headline.** A company with no owner cash cannot be valued on what it earns for its owners. It is valued on revenue', \
                'growth, and on profit measures that leave stock pay out. Where that revenue is held up by spending that has to go on,', \
                'such as a large sales force, a valuation on owner cash would be far lower. At the higher valuation its insiders are', \
                'granted shares, and can sell them, every year. The view of this index is that at some companies this is the point: owner', \
                'cash is kept near zero so that the company is never valued on it, while insiders turn grants into cash at the price that', \
                'results. Shareholders who take "investing in growth" on trust bear the cost. The numbers cannot say at which companies', \
                'this is deliberate: a real bet on growth looks the same until it pays off.', \
                '', \
                '**The three signs.** The index looks for three things together, each one visible in the filings. A real growth bet', \
                'loses them in time: owner cash appears as growth slows, costs leave part of gross profit, and insiders take out little.', \
                '', \
                '1. **Owner cash that stays near zero.** Together the 5 companies here recorded $6.8B of stock pay against $5.2B of free cash flow.', \
                '   At 5 of the 5, owner cash has been under 3.0% of revenue for five or more fiscal years running.', \
                '2. **Revenue held up by costs.** Operating costs are mostly people, and have to go on for the revenue to go on. At 1 of the 5,', \
                '   they took all of gross profit or more in the latest year. Cash that does arrive can go to buying back the shares just', \
                '   granted: of the 1 that bought back shares over five years and whose share count can be followed,', \
                '   the count still rose at 1, after $40.0M spent on buybacks and employees\' vesting taxes.', \
                [ \
                    '3. **Insiders who cash out.** In 2021‑2025, the officers and directors of 4 of these companies took $420.0M out through stock:', \
                    '   open-market sales and shares given up for taxes, less what they paid to buy shares and exercise options. That is 1.3% of those companies\' revenue over the same years.', \
